# Sample fixtures / corpus — NEVER ship real or synthetic malware/phishing.
# Keep generator scripts here in-repo (under tests/fixtures/) but ignore the
# generated artifacts. Run `python3 tests/fixtures/unicode_qr/generate.py`
# locally to regenerate.
tests/fixtures/**/qr_fixture*
tests/fixtures/**/*.lnk
tests/fixtures/**/*.url
tests/fixtures/**/*.ppkg
tests/fixtures/**/*.msc
tests/fixtures/**/*.rdp
tests/fixtures/**/*.eml
tests/fixtures/**/*.msg
tests/fixtures/**/*.chm
tests/fixtures/**/*.wps
tests/fixtures/**/corpus/

# Per-host runtime config — set REDTUSK_STATE_DIR, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, etc.
# locally; never commit. See deploy/docker/.env.example for the template.
#
# Match by SHAPE, not by one exact path. The single `deploy/docker/.env` rule below
# ignored precisely one filename, so an ordinary editing habit — `cp .env .env.bak`
# before a config experiment — produced `deploy/docker/.env.bak-tiertest`, which was
# untracked but NOT ignored and one `git add -A` away from committing live MinIO and
# Postgres credentials. The whole family is covered now; example templates are the
# only env files that belong in the repo.
# Dot-prefixed env files anywhere -- the classic secret shape. The separator class
# covers the real editing habits: .env.bak, .env-backup, .env~, .env.save. A bare
# `.env.*` missed the last three (review of PR #33).
.env
.env[-_.~]*

# deploy/ is where the per-host secrets actually live, so match aggressively HERE
# rather than repo-wide: an unanchored `*.env` hides legitimate compose env_file
# fragments and test fixtures elsewhere in the tree for no security gain.
deploy/**/*.env
deploy/**/*.env[-_.~]*
deploy/**/env[-_.~]*

# Example templates are the one env shape that belongs in the repo. Deliberately
# broad: `!*.env.example` only rescued the literal `.env.example` suffix, so a
# namespaced template like `.env.aws-burst.example` was silently dropped.
# Scoped to ENV templates specifically. A bare `!*.example` is repo-wide and would
# re-include fixture/log/bench files that earlier rules deliberately ignore.
!*.env*.example
# ...and the same for a template with NO leading dot, which `deploy/**/env[-_.~]*` above
# catches (`env.example`, `env.sample`). The negation demands a literal `.env` before
# `.example`, so `deploy/docker/env.example` was ignored and silently uncommittable --
# exactly the shape the comment block above says these rules exist to protect.
# Verified with `git check-ignore`; both spellings are now committable.
!**/env*.example
!**/env*.sample

# corpus-derived bench output — never commit
deploy/firecracker/bench_*.json

# stray tika NER model artifacts (maven dumps these in the wrong place)
/src/test/

# Local working checkouts of upstream Tika, used to build/compare the vendored parser jar.
# Never tracked, ~5k files / 4.4M lines each -- a stray `git add -A` swept .tika-src into a
# commit that was meant to touch six files, which is exactly how a scratch tree ends up in
# somebody's history. Ignore them so the mistake is not available.
.tika-src/
.tika-baseline/
.tika-fixed/
